Switching — Networking Notes ⭐
1. What is Switching?

Switching is the process of forwarding network data from one device to another within a network, mainly a LAN.

A network switch connects multiple devices such as computers, servers, printers, and other network devices.

PC1 ──┐
PC2 ──┼── Switch ── Server
PC3 ──┘

A switch mainly works at OSI Layer 2 (Data Link Layer) and uses MAC addresses to forward Ethernet frames.

2. Why Do We Need a Switch?

Without a switch, connecting many devices efficiently in a LAN would be difficult.

A switch provides:

Connectivity between devices
Efficient frame forwarding
Better use of network bandwidth
Communication between devices in the same LAN
Network segmentation using VLANs

3. MAC Address ⭐

A MAC address (Media Access Control address) is a hardware/network-interface address used for communication at Layer 2.

Example format:

00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E

A switch uses the destination MAC address in an Ethernet frame to determine where to forward the frame.

Remember
Switch → MAC Address
Router → IP Address

4. MAC Address Table ⭐

A switch maintains a MAC address table, also called a CAM table.

It maps:

MAC Address → Switch Port

For example, conceptually:

MAC A → Port 1
MAC B → Port 2
MAC C → Port 3

The switch uses this information to forward frames toward the correct port.

5. How Does a Switch Learn MAC Addresses?

When a frame enters a switch, the switch examines the source MAC address and learns which port that MAC address is associated with.

Basic Process
Frame arrives
     ↓
Read Source MAC
     ↓
Learn MAC → Port
     ↓
Read Destination MAC
     ↓
Forward frame

This learning process allows the switch to build its MAC address table dynamically.


10. VLAN — Virtual LAN ⭐⭐⭐

This is especially important for cloud/networking fundamentals.

A VLAN logically divides a physical switch network into separate broadcast domains.

Instead of having all devices in one large Layer 2 network, we can logically separate them.

Physical Switch
      |
 ┌────┼────┐
 ↓    ↓    ↓
VLAN10 VLAN20 VLAN30
Users Servers Guests
Why VLAN?
Network segmentation
Better security
Smaller broadcast domains
Better network organization
Traffic isolation


A VLAN logically divides a physical network into separate Layer 2 broadcast domains. It is commonly used for network segmentation, security, and better traffic management.


12. Switching and IP Address

A switch primarily makes forwarding decisions using MAC addresses, but IP addresses are still present in the packets carried inside Ethernet frames.

Think of the communication as:

Application Data
      ↓
IP Packet
      ↓
Ethernet Frame
      ↓
Switch uses MAC

This distinction is important when learning AWS networking, where you will frequently work with IP addresses, subnets, routing, and security controls.

13. Switch vs Hub ⭐

A hub sends incoming traffic to all connected ports.

A switch learns MAC addresses and can forward traffic toward the appropriate port.

Hub	Switch
Layer 1	Mainly Layer 2
Sends traffic to all ports	Uses MAC table for forwarding
Less efficient	More efficient
Mostly obsolete	Widely used

